WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.859 --> 00:00:05.260
Mjolnir has always been a huge part of Thor's
adventures, and "Thor: Love and Thunder" is

00:00:05.260 --> 00:00:06.510
certainly no exception.

00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:09.389
But what's the history behind Thor's magical
hammer?

00:00:09.389 --> 00:00:11.740
And what does "Mjolnir" even mean?

00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:13.780
Keep watching to find out.

00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:17.910
One of Mjolnir's most distinctive features
is its short handle, but there's a lot more

00:00:17.910 --> 00:00:20.500
to Mjolnir's length than you might expect.

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:24.600
The story behind Mjolnir's creation, as well
as its unique appearance, goes as far back

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:28.820
as the original Norse myths, and Loki's penchant
for mischief.

00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:33.871
In the midst of a much larger tale about Loki
stealing the hair of Thor's wife, Sif, Loki

00:00:33.871 --> 00:00:37.910
commissions two dwarfs to create three magical
gifts for the gods.

00:00:37.910 --> 00:00:41.940
To up the stakes, Loki bets his head that
the two couldn't make gifts as good as the

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:44.440
dwarfs who made Sif's replacement hair.

00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:48.870
To ensure that he keeps his head, Loki transforms
into a fly and bites one of the dwarfs while

00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:54.059
he makes Mjolnir, resulting in the hammer
coming out with a shorter handle than intended.

00:00:54.059 --> 00:00:58.020
Despite its top-heavy appearance, it was still
mighty and balanced, and the mythological

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:00.350
Thor cherished it all the same.

00:01:00.350 --> 00:01:04.820
While the mythological origins of Mjolnir
weren't carried over into Marvel perfectly

00:01:04.820 --> 00:01:08.991
intact, with the comic version being crafted
out of a fictional metal called Uru— in

00:01:08.991 --> 00:01:13.010
a dying star, in one version of the origin
story — it is interesting to see how the

00:01:13.010 --> 00:01:16.290
myths influenced the design in the comics.

00:01:16.290 --> 00:01:20.960
It was a major moment in "Avengers: Endgame"
when fans finally saw Captain America wield

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:25.780
Mjolnir, proving once and for all that he
truly is worthy of the power of Thor.

00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:31.530
"Whosoever holds this hammer, if he be worthy,
shall possess the power of Thor."

00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:35.580
It was a plot moment that was multiple movies
in the making, with Cap's worthiness being

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:38.700
teased way back at the beginning of "Avengers:
Age of Ultron."

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:43.979
However, as many Marvel megafans already know,
Captain America is far from the only superhero

00:01:43.979 --> 00:01:45.990
worthy of wielding the hammer.

00:01:45.990 --> 00:01:47.940
"I knew it!"

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:52.189
Aside from Cap and Vision, who briefly wielded
the hammer in "Age of Ultron," the Marvel

00:01:52.189 --> 00:01:56.650
Database actually lists dozens of characters
who are able to hold Mjolnir.

00:01:56.650 --> 00:02:01.220
These include other Asgardians like Odin and
Bor, familiar characters like Jane Foster

00:02:01.220 --> 00:02:05.350
and Black Panther, and even MCU up-and-comers
like Moon Knight.

00:02:05.350 --> 00:02:09.950
Those unfamiliar with Moon Knight's lore might
be confused as to how a somewhat unhinged

00:02:09.950 --> 00:02:13.750
vigilante with multiple personalities could
be worthy of Thor's hammer.

00:02:13.750 --> 00:02:19.420
However, thanks to Khonshu, the ancient Egyptian
moon god whom Moon Knight serves, Moon Knight

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:24.590
can hold and even control Mjolnir because
Uru is technically made of moon rock.

00:02:24.590 --> 00:02:29.250
Lightning, flying, and hitting things really
hard are the powers most people would associate

00:02:29.250 --> 00:02:30.420
with Mjolnir.

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:32.920
"What am I looking at?"

00:02:32.920 --> 00:02:37.430
"Sometimes it takes a while, give it a second."

00:02:37.430 --> 00:02:42.160
However, Mjolnir is capable of much more than
summoning lightning and smashing bad guys.

00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:47.260
Much like how Superman once had some odd powers
he no longer uses, Mjolnir has a similar tendency

00:02:47.260 --> 00:02:50.410
to do whatever the writer needs it to at that
moment.

00:02:50.410 --> 00:02:54.920
Just to name a few examples of this, Mjolnir
can open wormholes to instantly travel across

00:02:54.920 --> 00:02:59.620
the universe, turn Thor or others invisible,
manipulate people's souls, translate everything

00:02:59.620 --> 00:03:03.790
Thor says into any language he needs, and
sense energy signatures.

00:03:03.790 --> 00:03:07.870
Of course, none of those powers quite replace
bonking people on the head and shooting them

00:03:07.870 --> 00:03:11.950
with lightning in most situations, which is
why you still primarily see Thor using those

00:03:11.950 --> 00:03:13.599
abilities in battle.

00:03:13.599 --> 00:03:17.209
Nevertheless, it's important to remember that
Mjolnir is still one of the most powerful

00:03:17.209 --> 00:03:19.170
and versatile weapons in the universe.

00:03:19.170 --> 00:03:23.959
Therefore, Thor is likely much more versatile
than most comics and MCU fans would give him

00:03:23.959 --> 00:03:24.959
credit for.

00:03:24.959 --> 00:03:28.260
He's certainly more than just a hunk of muscle
and lightning.

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:31.930
A lot has happened to both Thor and Mjolnir
in recent comics.

00:03:31.930 --> 00:03:36.250
From becoming unworthy and allowing the mantle
of Thor to be carried by Jane Foster, to seeing

00:03:36.250 --> 00:03:41.140
Jane sacrifice both herself and the hammer,
to finally reforging the hammer so that Thor

00:03:41.140 --> 00:03:45.590
could use it in the War of the Realms, Mjolnir
has seen a lot of change.

00:03:45.590 --> 00:03:49.460
What nobody ever expected, however, was for
the hammer to wake up.

00:03:49.460 --> 00:03:52.430
Of course, Mjolnir had always been somewhat
alive.

00:03:52.430 --> 00:03:56.600
One version of its comic-book origin story
reveals that Odin trapped the God Tempest

00:03:56.600 --> 00:03:59.099
in the Uru that would become Mjolnir.

00:03:59.099 --> 00:04:04.159
Upon being reforged, however, the God Tempest's
essence combined with that of Mangog, a being

00:04:04.159 --> 00:04:07.270
who despised Asgard for the genocide of its
people.

00:04:07.270 --> 00:04:12.400
Thus, Mjolnir was able to create its own avatar,
one that no longer cared for Thor or Asgard

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:17.630
and only desired death and destruction, dubbing
itself the "God of Hammers."

00:04:17.630 --> 00:04:21.800
Mjolnir showed some cool new abilities in
"Thor: Love and Thunder," including the power

00:04:21.800 --> 00:04:26.780
to reassemble itself from its shattered Uru
metal shards — and then temporarily disassemble

00:04:26.780 --> 00:04:29.500
itself again to attack an enemy from all directions.

00:04:29.500 --> 00:04:34.120
But did you know those pieces of Mjolnir can
become entirely new hammers in the comics?

00:04:34.120 --> 00:04:38.740
This came in handy for Puddlegulp, one of
Thor's more unusual friends, who first appeared

00:04:38.740 --> 00:04:40.839
in "Thor" #364.

00:04:40.839 --> 00:04:45.559
Once a man by the name of Simon Walterson
who was transformed into a frog by a curse,

00:04:45.559 --> 00:04:50.559
"Puddlegulp" was adopted by New York's secret
frog kingdom and fought against a rival rat

00:04:50.559 --> 00:04:54.949
army alongside Thor when the Thunder God was
transformed into a frog by Loki.

00:04:54.949 --> 00:04:59.869
Puddlegulp revealed his true identity to Thor
and watched as the Frog of Thunder lifted

00:04:59.869 --> 00:05:03.249
Mjolnir, transforming into a giant frog in
Thor's armor.

00:05:03.249 --> 00:05:08.180
Unknown to Thor, a sliver of Mjolnir had broken
off the hammer, and when Puddlegulp proved

00:05:08.180 --> 00:05:13.779
worthy enough to lift the shard of Uru metal,
it transformed into the mighty Frogjolnir

00:05:13.779 --> 00:05:18.050
and turned the enchanted frog into Throg,
Frog of Thunder.

00:05:18.050 --> 00:05:22.819
Throg used his Asgardian powers to protect
the frogs of Central Park and eventually went

00:05:22.819 --> 00:05:27.189
on to team up with other animal superhero
sidekicks to form "The Pet Avengers."

00:05:27.189 --> 00:05:31.990
Weird as this story might sound, if Mjolnir
really is a sentient being, it stands to reason

00:05:31.990 --> 00:05:35.960
that it can reproduce and make new enchanted
hammers for the worthy.

00:05:35.960 --> 00:05:36.960
Including frogs.

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:41.740
Fans have been struggling with the proper
pronunciation of "Mjolnir" for decades.

00:05:41.740 --> 00:05:45.419
Once you figure it out, however, an even more
relevant question comes up.

00:05:45.419 --> 00:05:47.580
What does "Mjolnir" actually mean?

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:48.870
Let's explore!

00:05:48.870 --> 00:05:51.460
"Let's bring the rainbow!"

00:05:51.460 --> 00:05:56.099
Most scholars trace the name "Mjolnir" back
to Old Slavic, Russian, and Welsh terms which

00:05:56.099 --> 00:05:58.409
all translate into the word "lightning."

00:05:58.409 --> 00:06:03.629
As Thor's own name goes back to a Proto-Germanic
root meaning "Thunder," this seems appropriate.

00:06:03.629 --> 00:06:08.270
However, some scholars also point out that
"Mjolnir" may be related to the Icelandic

00:06:08.270 --> 00:06:12.550
words meaning "new snow" and "white," both
symbols of purity.

00:06:12.550 --> 00:06:16.710
This could relate to the way Thor's hammer
was often used in rituals like marriage, effectively

00:06:16.710 --> 00:06:19.169
purifying those it blessed.

00:06:19.169 --> 00:06:24.080
Mjolnir may also be derived from a Proto-Germanic
word which translates into "to grind."

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:28.080
This could mean Mjolnir was known as "the
grinder" or "the crusher" — two things it's

00:06:28.080 --> 00:06:31.639
still known for doing today in the Marvel
Universe.

00:06:31.639 --> 00:06:36.659
Thor variants show up in multiple fictional
universes, and DC Comics has its own Thor

00:06:36.659 --> 00:06:38.759
who wields his own mighty hammer.

00:06:38.759 --> 00:06:43.659
However, it's actually Marvel's version of
Mjolnir that caused two DC icons — Superman

00:06:43.659 --> 00:06:47.050
and Wonder Woman — to share a special bond.

00:06:47.050 --> 00:06:52.479
Superman got to wield Mjolnir in the DC/Marvel
crossover miniseries "JLA/Avengers."

00:06:52.479 --> 00:06:57.429
The story saw Marvel's Avengers and DC's Justice
League come to blows and eventually team-up

00:06:57.429 --> 00:07:00.419
to save their universes from mutual destruction.

00:07:00.419 --> 00:07:04.539
In the final battle, Thor passed his hammer
to Superman, who found his power magnified

00:07:04.539 --> 00:07:07.020
beyond imagination by Mjolnir's magic.

00:07:07.020 --> 00:07:11.439
Curiously, the Man of Steel found he could
no longer lift the hammer following the battle,

00:07:11.439 --> 00:07:16.059
although Thor assured him Mjolnir was in good
hands for the brief time he wielded it.

00:07:16.059 --> 00:07:20.639
Wonder Woman found herself worthy to lift
Mjolnir in the "DC vs. Marvel" crossover event

00:07:20.639 --> 00:07:25.369
when she came across it following Thor's battle
with Captain Marvel/Shazam.

00:07:25.369 --> 00:07:29.689
Although the hammer increased Wonder Woman's
power to insane levels, Diana decided to fight

00:07:29.689 --> 00:07:33.819
without it when she was matched up with Storm,
feeling Mjolnir's magic would give her an

00:07:33.819 --> 00:07:35.300
unfair advantage.

00:07:35.300 --> 00:07:39.690
Later, she politely handed the hammer back
to a perplexed Thor.

00:07:39.690 --> 00:07:43.969
Although these crossover events would fade
in and out of memory, both Superman and Wonder

00:07:43.969 --> 00:07:48.580
Woman are remembered as worthy wielders of
Mjolnir in "Thor: The Worthy" when Jane Foster's

00:07:48.580 --> 00:07:53.370
Thor glimpses the Man of Steel and Amazon
Princess among the worthy.

00:07:53.370 --> 00:07:58.289
Superman himself indicates he recalls these
events in "Superman" Vol. 2 #165 when he gives

00:07:58.289 --> 00:08:04.423
Diana a gold pendant shaped like Mjolnir as
a Christmas gift, recognizing their unique bond.

00:08:05.275 --> 00:08:09.969
Mjolnir is a mighty weapon — but some fans
forget that it's also something far more than

00:08:09.969 --> 00:08:10.969
just a weapon.

00:08:10.969 --> 00:08:16.139
Since the Asgardians were once worshiped as
gods, Mjolnir is considered a religious item,

00:08:16.139 --> 00:08:21.610
meaning it is particularly dangerous against
supernatural entities like vampires and the undead.

00:08:22.770 --> 00:08:25.099
"Hey Blade!"

00:08:25.099 --> 00:08:30.569
In "Thor" #332, fans see just why vampires
should be afraid of Mjolnir, when Thor goes

00:08:30.569 --> 00:08:34.030
up against some bloodsucking fiends serving
Dracula.

00:08:34.030 --> 00:08:38.430
Upon seeing a vampire attack a woman, Thor
throws his hammer at him, causing the creature

00:08:38.430 --> 00:08:42.810
to burst into flame and transform into dust
the second Mjolnir touches him.

00:08:42.810 --> 00:08:48.447
The hammer proves equally effective when Thor
dusts some giant vampire bats a few panels later.

00:08:48.447 --> 00:08:52.759
Unfortunately, when Thor attempts to use Mjolnir
against Dracula himself in the next issue,

00:08:52.759 --> 00:08:56.110
the vampire lord gets teleported away.

00:08:56.110 --> 00:09:00.680
As is the case with a lot of godlike superheroes,
Thor possesses the power of flight.

00:09:00.680 --> 00:09:05.160
Unlike Superman or Wonder Woman, however,
the God of Thunder has a very specific means

00:09:05.160 --> 00:09:10.480
of getting airborne — he spins Mjolnir really
fast and then launches it skyward, using its

00:09:10.480 --> 00:09:13.220
forward momentum to pull him into the air.

00:09:13.220 --> 00:09:17.649
As it turns out, this unique means of "flight"
was devised by Stan Lee to get around what

00:09:17.649 --> 00:09:22.769
he saw as a common fallacy among flying superheroes
— they had no visible means of propulsion.

00:09:22.769 --> 00:09:28.010
In one of his videos for "Stan's Rants," Lee
revealed that when Thor wants to fly, quote,

00:09:28.010 --> 00:09:33.440
"we use a scientific principle," explaining
how his use of "hammer propulsion" provided

00:09:33.440 --> 00:09:36.730
a scientifically sound explanation for Thor's
flight.

00:09:36.730 --> 00:09:41.800
Over the years, however, various artists have
shown Thor flying without Mjolnir in Superman-type

00:09:41.800 --> 00:09:45.990
poses, calling into question whether Thor
actually needs his hammer to fly.

00:09:45.990 --> 00:09:50.530
Nevertheless, science writer Kyle Hill proposes
in his "Scientific American" article "The

00:09:50.530 --> 00:09:55.269
God of Thunder, and Momentum," that Mjolnir
could conceivably drag a much lighter Thor

00:09:55.269 --> 00:10:00.279
through space if it made itself incredibly
heavy (something that also explains how Mjolnir

00:10:00.279 --> 00:10:02.540
could keep the unworthy from lifting it).

00:10:02.540 --> 00:10:05.120
Who knew Stan Lee was such a science genius?

00:10:05.120 --> 00:10:06.269
"That's it.

00:10:06.269 --> 00:10:07.490
No more rant.

00:10:07.490 --> 00:10:08.490
Goodbye."

00:10:08.490 --> 00:10:10.060
Check out one of our newest videos right here!

00:10:10.060 --> 00:10:13.040
Plus, even more Looper videos about the MCU
are coming soon.

